title:  Year 7 Enrichment Mathematics
paper:  Measurement Test
year:   Year 7
time:   40 minutes
footer: Show all mathematical procedures.
describe: In-class test on measurement which will allow you to show your skills in understanding, fluency, problem-solving and reasoning.
covers: Calculate the circumference of a circle and the arc length/perimeter of a sector.
covers: Establish and use the formulas for the areas of rectangles, triangles and composite shapes.
covers: Calculate the volumes of rectangular and triangular prisms, and apply these skills to unfamiliar and real-world contexts.
rule:   You will complete the assessment task, individually, under direct supervision.
rule:   Time allowed is 40 minutes.
rule:   You are allowed a calculator.
rule:   You are required to read each question and write your answers in the spaces provided, showing all mathematical procedures.

section: 1 — Foundational skills

Q Calculate the volume of each rectangular prism shown.
a [C] (2)
fig prism l=6 w=4 h=3
b [C] (2)
fig prism l=9 w=5 h=4
c [C] (2)
fig prism l=3 w=1.2 h=0.4 unit=m

Q Calculate the area of each shape shown.
a [C] (2) Rectangle
fig rect w=8 h=5
b [C] (2) Triangle
fig tri b=10 h=4

Q A circle has a diameter of 14 cm. A sector of a different circle has radius 6 cm and an angle of 120°.
a [C] (2) Calculate the circumference of the circle, correct to 1 decimal place.
fig circle d=14
b [C] (2) Calculate the arc length of the sector, correct to 1 decimal place.
fig sector r=6 angle=120

section: 2 — Applied skills

Q A garden bed is shaped like a sector of radius 8 m and an angle of 100°, as shown below. Fencing costs $24 per metre.
fig sector r=8 angle=100 side
a [B] (3) Calculate the perimeter of the garden bed, correct to 1 decimal place.
b [B] (2) Calculate the cost of fencing the garden bed.

Q A raised garden bed is built in the shape of the rectangular prism shown, and is to be completely filled with soil. Soil is sold in bags, each containing 0.5 m³ of soil.
fig prism l=2 w=1.5 h=0.5 unit=m side
a [B] (2) Calculate the volume of soil needed to fill the garden bed.
b [B] (2) Determine how many full bags of soil must be purchased.

Q A carpenter is building the L-shaped timber deck shown below. Decking boards cost $48 per square metre.
fig lshape w=8 h=6 cutW=3 cutH=2 unit=m side
a [B] (3) Calculate the total area of the deck.
b [B] (2) Calculate the total cost of the decking boards needed to cover the deck.

section: 3 — Extension

Q [A] (5) space=55 A tent is built in the shape of a triangular prism, as shown below. The base of the triangular cross-section is 4 m, and the tent has a length of 6 m. The volume of the tent is 84 m³. Calculate the height, h, of the triangular cross-section.
fig triprism b=4 h=h l=6 unit=m side

Q A logging truck is loaded with cylindrical logs. Each log is 10 m long and has the circular cross-section shown, and the timber has a density of 850 kg per cubic metre. The truck's legal load limit is 12 000 kg.
fig circle r=0.3 unit=m side
note The area of a circle can be found using the formula A = πr², where r is the radius.
a [A] (2) Calculate the cross-sectional area of one log, correct to 2 decimal places.
b [A] (6) space=60 Using the fact that Volume = cross-sectional area × length (the same relationship used for prisms), calculate the volume of one log. The truck is loaded with 6 of these logs: determine the total mass of the load, and state, with justification, whether the truck would be within its legal weight limit.

Q [A] (4) space=55 The diagram shows a 3D composite solid: a rectangular-prism base with a triangular-prism roof on top. Calculate the total volume of the solid.
fig houseprism w=8 h=4 l=10 rh=3 unit=m side
